WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.800
For navigation software like AvNav to function, it necessarily needs data.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:18.760
This video aims to demonstrate how AvNav processes different types of navigation data. It is intended

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.000
for beginners and users who want to understand and implement common data traffic scenarios with AvNav

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.880
on board. In fact, processing

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.200
data with AvNav can be a complex undertaking requiring in-depth training

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.320
if you want to explore all its features and possibilities. The corresponding

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:50.240
articles in the documentation, in the section for specialists, provide further information. Let's begin by considering which devices

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:56.880
typically provide navigation data for AvNav on board.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:04.800
This is usually GPS, primarily for position, SOG, and time.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.840
We then receive water depth from an echo sounder, data for

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.440
relative wind speed and angle from a wind sensor,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:20.240
and the position data of other vessels transmitted via the AIS system.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:27.040
AvNav can not only process incoming data but also output processed data.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.400
This is particularly relevant for feeding data to autopilots or secondary displays.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.400
This is intended to describe the minimum amount of data

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:42.240
assumed in this video. The devices described deliver their

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:51.480
data in different formats. These are typically NMEA0183, NMEA2000, and Seatalk.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.720
To make the data available to AvNav in these different formats, hardware is needed,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:04.640
specifically converters or interfaces, because depending on the computer AvNav is running on,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:11.760
the data must reach AvNav somehow. Let's start with the simplest case:

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:21.560
GPS data from a GPS mouse should reach AvNav. We'll use a GPS mouse that

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:28.200
delivers NMEA0183 data. That clarifies the format. A USB connector is at the end of the cable. This

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.480
also clarifies the interface; it's USB, i.e., a serial interface.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.240
On Linux systems, AvNav automatically detects when data

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.960
is arriving at one of the serial interfaces and sets up so-called readers in the Connections section. On the software side, they configure the

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:53.520
system so that the NMEA0183 data can reach AvNav and

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.600
be used for navigation. On Android systems, these connections must

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:09.720
be set up manually. This was manageable and quite simple. With a fish finder that provides NMEA0183 data

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.840
or a marine radio that transmits AIS data, it's a bit different, because you

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.000
'll usually only find open cable ends on these devices. These devices typically use the

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.960
RS485 or RS232 interface. Like USB, these are also serial interfaces.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:38.080
Hardware versions are available as add-on boards or HATs for a Raspberry Pi, or as USB adapters.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:44.160
This is where it gets a bit more complicated, because hardware needs to be installed.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.120
You can find instructions on how to do this in the documentation for the device you're using.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.280
A third option is via Wi-Fi, as used, for example, by the open-source wind sensor

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.920
"Yachta" from the Open Boat Project. However, this requires an onboard network

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.640
that provides Wi-Fi. For instructions on how to do this with AvNav or an additional

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:13.320
Wi-Fi router, please refer to the documentation. Two things are important here:

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:21.560
First, data in NMEA0183 format can only be transmitted directly from device to device. This means that

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.040
each sending device needs its own converter or an input on the receiver,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.840
i.e., the AvNav computer. Alternatively, it needs its own network address.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.360
Second, it's important to know that, for licensing reasons, AvNav itself only

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:42.320
processes NMEA0183 data. However, most recreational boats nowadays use

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:50.360
NMEA2000 networks. On the CAN bus used there, any

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.960
connected device can act as both a sender and a receiver. Therefore, to integrate your AvNav computer into such a

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.240
CAN bus system, you will definitely need a converter

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:10.600
that transforms NMEA2000 data into NMEA0183 for AvNav and vice versa.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:19.840
Unlike NMEA0183, the number of devices connected to the NMEA2000 bus is irrelevant.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.240
Several types of converters are suitable: one being the

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:32.960
N2K gateway, specifically developed for AvNav, which converts the NMEA2000 data and transmits it via WLAN and USB.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:39.840
With a plug-in add-on module, the data can also be converted to RS485.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.920
This hardware option is well-suited for

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:50.880
integrating an Android-based AvNav into an NMEA2000 network. This allows

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:57.360
the use of converters from various other manufacturers with different functionalities. A brief overview is available here:

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.920
https://open-boat-projects.org/de/nmea2k-wlan-bridge-eine-marktuebersicht/

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:05.400
CAN adapters, such as those

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:15.280
found on HAT boards for Raspberry Pis or USB devices (e.g., from Actisense), which do not convert to NMEA0183 themselves, can also be integrated. The conversion

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:22.280
to and from NMEA0183 is then performed via additional software on the headless AvNav images, called CanBoat.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:29.320
Depending on the data format and

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:37.360
hardware used, certain data can also leave AvNav: Via NMEA2000 converters and adapters,

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:44.800
serial interfaces, or LAN and WLAN, AvNav provides NMEA0183 data, primarily for

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:50.600
supplying an autopilot. Please refer to the documentation for

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:57.040
details on which specific data sets are available . If you

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:02.440
want more flexibility in handling data, and especially if you want to send more than just autopilot data,

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:10.160
a second powerful component comes into play, which is part of the headless image: the SignalK server.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:16.000
It is already connected to AvNav and can

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.960
add a great deal of additional functionality to the entire system through its plugins. Unfortunately, it does not run on Android systems.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:27.840
If you would like to delve deeper into the SignalK server, please consult the relevant

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:34.400
manuals; otherwise, this video would become considerably too long.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:40.200
Just a brief overview of SignalK and AvNav, which is what makes the whole thing quite complex:

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:47.040
AvNav and SignalK are linked via several data formats. You can therefore

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:54.120
assign all the data connections described above to the SignalK server and configure them from there.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:08:00.520
This is how it's done on the open-source system Openplotter. However, you then have to accept

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:07.400
that a SignalK server failure will also take down the data connections to AvNav.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:12.480
Mixed configurations are also possible, meaning you can

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:19.680
feed some of your data directly into AvNav and another part via the SignalK server. This can

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.680
be useful if alarm messages are generated in the SignalK server and

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:32.720
are to be output via AvNav. Similarly, apps like Node-RED or KIP, which are installed via the SignalK server, can

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:38.480
be used to process and display data. The data for this can then

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:46.040
come from AvNav or be sent to AvNav. It sounds complicated, and it is. With such

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:51.360
mixed systems, it's extremely important to structure the generated data flow in such a way

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.920
that you don't create a loop. That is, to avoid repeatedly sending the same data back and forth between SignalK and

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:04.640
AvNav. This would significantly slow down the entire system.

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:11.840
So, if you're planning something like this, you can use filters. Filters allow you

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:19.240
to control the sending and receiving of data. This works in both the SignalK server and AvNav.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.390
Therefore, you should know exactly what you're doing. And before you begin, work through the chapters in the documentation that

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:24.720
explain all the data traffic in AvNav. The flexibility in configuring

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.960
AvNav's data traffic, with or without the SignalK server, offers many possibilities,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:39.320
but can also cause some problems. Fortunately, as the user, you're in control.

00:09:42.288 --> 00:09:49.180
That's it. Have fun setting up your data connections, and above all, good luck!

